  "releaseNotes": "Features\n\nSpans in properties, indexers and events (part of #201). These no longer report IMP009:\n\nProperties of type Span\\<T> or ReadOnlySpan\\<T> (#224). The imposter keeps the value as the array of its elements, and Returns takes that array.\n\nIndexers whose keys or value are spans (#248). Keys are matched with SpanArg\\<T>/ReadOnlySpanArg\\<T>.\n\nEvents whose delegate takes a span by value (#260). Raising one copies the span's elements into the history, and Raised matches them with SpanArg\\<T>/ReadOnlySpanArg\\<T>.\n\nStill IMP009 (see Limitations):\n\ncustom ref structs;\n\nspans returned by reference;\n\na span an async event's delegate takes, or one an event's delegate takes by ref or out;\n\nthe scoped cases;\n\ntype parameters that allow ref structs.\n\nNew diagnostics\n\nEach of these targets used to get an imposter that didn't compile, with no diagnostic. Now it reports an error and gets no imposter:\n\nIMP011: a method, property or indexer that returns by ref or ref readonly (#244, closes #216).\n\nIMP012: an interface with a static abstract member that the interface doesn't implement (#223, closes #217).\n\nIMP009: a method whose type parameter has the C# 13 allows ref struct anti-constraint (#222, closes #218).\n\nFixes\n\nClasses with required members (#219, closes #215). Their imposters now compile and leave the required members at their defaults.\n\nStatic members of interface targets (#221, closes #220). They're no longer impersonated. A static member is always called through the interface, so its setup never took effect.\n\nEvent raise methods and Raised keep the delegate's nullable annotations (#265, closes #258). For an EventHandler, Raise(null, EventArgs.Empty) no longer warns CS8625.\n\nA generic method parameter named it (#245, closes #241). Its arguments criteria no longer fail with CS1061.\n\nSame-named targets of different arity on C# 14, such as IFoo and IFoo\\<T> (#246, closes #242). Their Imposter() extension classes no longer collide.\n\nA target type parameter named TException (#268, closes #264). The builders' Throws\\<TException>() no longer shadows it (CS0693).\n\nChanges to be aware of\n\nArg\\<object> for an object? event parameter now warns. With nullable reference types enabled, passing Arg\\<object> where the delegate's parameter is object? (an EventHandler's sender, for example) warns CS8620, as it already did for methods. Use Arg\\<object?>. The same applies to handler criteria for delegates such as Func\\<object?, EventArgs, Task>.\n\nSetups for an interface's static members are gone (see #221). They compiled but never had an effect.\n\nInternal\n\nThe generator's code was reorganised without changing what it generates. The snapshot tests confirm the output is byte-identical:\n\nshared builder helpers and metadata (#249, #250, #251, #253, #259, #263, #266, #267, #269, #270, #271, #274, #276, #277);\n\nnames read from metadata instead of string literals (#254, #256, #261);\n\nlong builder methods split up (#257, #272, #273, #275);\n\none vocabulary for the method feature's invocation imposter groups (#278);\n\ndead code removed (#247, #255);\n\ncomments and the IMP009 description brought up to date (#252).",
